                <h2>Account Suspension ≠ Account Death</h2>
<p>Many advertisers panic when they see the "Account suspended" email. They either repeatedly submit the same appeal content, or simply give up. In reality, Google Ads account appeals have clear material requirements and processes. With proper preparation, the success rate of appeals can be significantly improved.</p>
<p>This article organizes actionable checklists across four phases: <strong>Diagnosis → Preparation → Submission → Follow-up</strong>.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Figure Out "Why You Were Suspended"</h2>
<p>You must identify the problem before appealing. If you get this wrong, all subsequent preparation is wasted effort.</p>
<h3>📋 Violation Diagnosis Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Log into Google Ads backend and check "Account Settings → Policy Violations" for specific violation details</li>
<li>[ ] Distinguish between "System Automated Detection" and "Manual Review": the former usually comes with violation detail links, while the latter often indicates serious violations</li>
<li>[ ] If it's "Circumventing Systems" or "Abusive Behavior," these are harder to appeal and require significant preparation</li>
<li>[ ] Record the time of violation, ad campaign, and specific creatives for later correlation analysis</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Common Violation Types Reference</strong>: Misrepresentation, intellectual property complaints, unapproved sensitive categories (finance, healthcare), inaccessible landing pages, circumventing policy behavior.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Prepare the Appeal Letter</h2>
<p>The appeal letter is the core of your entire submission and determines the reviewer's first judgment.</p>
<h3>✉️ Essential Elements of an Appeal Letter</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Opening with clear request</strong>: State that the account/ad was suspended and apply for re-review</li>
<li><strong>Acknowledge the issue (if applicable)</strong>: If it's a clear violation, don't argue—be sincere</li>
<li><strong>Explain corrective measures taken</strong>: For example, "Removed violating creatives," "Modified landing page," "Submitted qualification documents"</li>
<li><strong>Commit to future compliance</strong>: Demonstrate your understanding and execution mechanism for Google Ads policies</li>
</ol>
<h3>⚠️ Appeal Letter Writing Pitfalls to Avoid</h3>
<ul>
<li>❌ Don't repeatedly submit the same appeal content—systems will flag it as "invalid appeal"</li>
<li>❌ Don't use emotional language ("Your judgment is unfair")</li>
<li>❌ Don't just say "Please review my account" without substantial content</li>
<li>✅ Use professional, concise business tone</li>
<li>✅ Keep it within 300-500 words</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Appeal Letter Template Framework</strong>:</p>
<pre><code>1. Account ID + violating campaign/ad
2. Our understanding of the violation reason
3. Corrective measures we have taken (listed point by point)
4. Our commitment to future compliance mechanisms
5. Request for re-review with contact information
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Prepare Supporting Document Materials</h2>
<p>Supporting materials vary depending on the violation type.</p>
<h3>📎 Attachment Checklist by Violation Type</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Violation Type</th>
<th>Required Materials</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Misrepresentation/Fraud</strong></td>
<td>Authentic product descriptions, third-party certifications, user review screenshots</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>IP Complaints</strong></td>
<td>Trademark/copyright authorization, original proof, DMCA counter-notice (if applicable)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Sensitive Categories (Finance/Health)</strong></td>
<td>Relevant regulatory licenses, industry qualifications, product compliance declarations</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Landing Page Issues</strong></td>
<td>Modified landing page URLs, page screenshots, design comparison</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Circumventing Systems</strong></td>
<td>Historical投放data explanation, evidence of misjudgment (if any), proof of violating creatives deleted</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>📂 Attachment Format Requirements</h3>
<ul>
<li>Convert all documents to PDF format to ensure cross-platform readability</li>
<li>Name files clearly, for example: "Business_License_2024.pdf"</li>
<li>Keep total attachment size per email under 10MB</li>
<li>If materials are extensive, use compressed files or provide Google Drive links</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Submit Your Appeal Correctly</h2>
<h3>⌨️ Submission Channel Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Submit through Google Ads backend "Help → Contact Us → Account Suspended" portal</li>
<li>[ ] Select the correct account (watch out for wrong account selection in multi-account management)</li>
<li>[ ] Choose the same language as the violation email (usually English)</li>
<li>[ ] Record the ticket number after submission for follow-up tracking</li>
</ul>
<h3>⏰ Review Time Reference</h3>
<ul>
<li>System automated violations: Usually 1-3 business days</li>
<li>Manual review: Usually 5-10 business days</li>
<li>Complex cases (circumventing behavior): May take 15-30 business days</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Wait at least 7 business days before following up or submitting a second appeal.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Post-Appeal Follow-up Strategy</h2>
<h3>📞 Follow-up Checklist</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] If no response after 10 business days, send a follow-up review request through the backend</li>
<li>[ ] If the first appeal was rejected, carefully read the rejection reason and supplement materials specifically</li>
<li>[ ] When submitting a second appeal, avoid repeating the same content—focus on new evidence</li>
<li>[ ] In extreme cases, seek help through Google Ads official Twitter (@GoogleAds) or Google Business Center</li>
</ul>
<h3>🔄 Second Appeal Material Upgrade Suggestions</h3>
<ol>
<li>Add third-party authoritative endorsements (testing reports, industry certifications)</li>
<li>Provide before/after comparison screenshots of remediation</li>
<li>Attach detailed compliance operation process documentation</li>
<li>If necessary, contact a Google Ads partner specialist for assistance</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Checklist: Review Before Submitting</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Logged into Google Ads to confirm specific violation reason</li>
<li>[ ] Appeal letter includes four parts: request, reason, remediation, commitment</li>
<li>[ ] Supporting materials match the violation type</li>
<li>[ ] All attachments converted to PDF with standardized naming</li>
<li>[ ] Submitted through correct portal with ticket number recorded</li>
<li>[ ] Set up follow-up reminder (7-10 business days later)</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>Account suspension is frustrating, but in most cases, it's not irreversible. The key is: <strong>Diagnose first, then remediate, then appeal</strong>—avoid blind submissions that waste time.</p>
<p>If your account involves multiple campaigns or complex violations, it's recommended to first clarify the logic line between violations and remediation before appealing. This significantly improves the reviewer's trust.</p>
<p>For more account appeal and advertising risk control tips, stay tuned for updates from 以渔数媒's operations guide.</p>
